1️⃣ Why String is immutable

A. Security
    Strings are heavily used in security-sensitive areas:
    * Database URLs
    * File paths
    * Network connections
    * Class loading
    * Usernames & passwords

     Example problem if String were mutable
    String url = "jdbc:mysql://localhost:3306/db";

    If String were mutable:
    * Another reference could change the value
    * DB connection could be redirected to a malicious server

    ❗ Because Strings are immutable:
    * Once created → no one can change it
    * safely reuse the same String

B. Performance (String Pool)
    Java maintains a String Constant Pool.

    String s1 = "Java";
    String s2 = "Java";
    Only one object is created.

    If String were mutable:
    * One reference changing it would affect all references
    * Pooling would be impossible

    ❗Immutability makes String Pool memory-efficient & fast

C. Thread Safety
    * Immutable objects are naturally thread-safe
    * Multiple threads can use the same String without synchronization

D. Hashing & Collections
    Strings are commonly used as keys in HashMap

    HashMap<String, Integer> map = new HashMap<>();

    If String were mutable:
    * Hash value would change
    * Object would become unreachable

    ❗Immutability ensures hashcode consistency

---
Summary: Why String is immutable

| Reason        | Benefit                            |
| ------------- | ---------------------------------- |
| Security      | Prevents unauthorized modification |
| Performance   | Enables String Pool                |
| Thread safety | No synchronization needed          |
| Hashing       | Stable hashcode                    |

