Heap vs String Constant Pool

🔹 String Constant Pool (SCP)

    * Special memory inside Heap
    * Stores String literals
    * Avoids duplicate objects

    String s1 = "Test";
    String s2 = "Test";
    String Constant Pool
    ┌─────────────┐
    │ "Test"      │◄── s1, s2
    └─────────────┘

🔹 Heap Memory

    * Stores objects created using `new`
    * Always creates a new object

    String s3 = new String("Test");

    Heap
    ┌──────────────────────┐
    │ new String("Test")   │◄── s3
    └──────────────────────┘


🔹 Combined Diagram

               Heap Memory
    ┌────────────────────────────┐
    │  String Constant Pool      │
    │  ┌─────────────┐           │
    │  │ "Test"      │◄─ s1, s2  │
    │  └─────────────┘           │
    │                            │
    │  new String("Test") ◄─ s3  │
    └────────────────────────────┘


⚠️ Important Interview Points
* SCP is part of Heap (since Java 7)

🔹 Before Java 7 (Java 6 and earlier)
   Where was SCP?
    👉 PermGen (Permanent Generation)
    SCP was stored in PermGen
    PermGen had fixed size
    Not cleaned properly by Garbage Collector

    ❌ Problems
    OutOfMemoryError: PermGen space
    Large number of string literals could crash the JVM
    Hard to tune memory

    Diagram (Java ≤ 6)
    JVM Memory
    ┌──────────────────────┐
    │ Heap                 │
    │  (Objects)           │
    └──────────────────────┘
    ┌──────────────────────┐
    │ PermGen              │
    │  └─ String Pool      │
    │  └─ Class Metadata   │
    └──────────────────────┘

🔹 Java 7
    What changed?
    👉 String Constant Pool moved from PermGen to Heap
    Why?
    👉 Heap is larger & flexible, Better Garbage Collection, Reduced memory leaks, Improved performance

    Diagram (Java 7+)
    Heap Memory
    ┌────────────────────────────┐
    │ String Constant Pool       │
    │ ┌─────────────┐            │
    │ │ "Hello"     │◄── s1, s2  │
    │ └─────────────┘            │
    │                            │
    │ new String("Hello") ◄─ s3  │
    └────────────────────────────┘

🔹 Java 8 and above (Another change)
   What happened to PermGen?
    ❌ PermGen removed completely

    Replaced by:
    👉 Metaspace

    Feature	        | PermGen	    | Metaspace
    Memory location	| JVM memory	| Native memory
    Auto resize	    | ❌ No	        |✅ Yes
    OOM issues	    | Common	    | Rare
    Stores Strings	|❌ No	        |❌ No

    ⚠️ SCP stays in Heap, not in Metaspace.
------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
🔹 Summary of Changes
Java Version	SCP Location
Java ≤ 6	    PermGen
Java 7	        Heap (moved)
Java 8+	        Heap (unchanged)

🔹 Why moving SCP to Heap was important (Interview Gold ⭐)
Better GC support
No PermGen OOM due to strings
More memory available
Simpler JVM tuning
Improved application stability

🔹 Common Interview Q&A
Q: Is SCP part of Metaspace?
❌ No
✔ SCP is part of Heap

Q: Does new String("A") store object in SCP?
❌ No
✔ Object in Heap, literal "A" in SCP

Q: What does intern() do?
✔ Moves/returns string reference from SCP (Heap in Java 7+)

🔑 One-line Interview Answer
Before Java 7, SCP was in PermGen causing memory issues.
From Java 7 onward, SCP moved to Heap for better GC and scalability.
Java 8 removed PermGen entirely and introduced Metaspace.

